WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:03.900
Hey Crazies.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.760
I was thinking lately, I’m an astrophysicist
and I haven’t done nearly enough

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.000
astronomy videos.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.740
Imposter Syndrome is probably partly to blame.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.320
Stop self-diagnosing, Nick.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.460
Anyway, we’re going to break this pattern
right meow!!

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.500
Our last video was on nuclear fusion,
which usually happens in the cores of stars,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.980
so let’s talk about stars!!!

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.580
There is way too much about stars to cram
into a one video

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.740
so we’ll focus on a specific obstacle:

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.800
How do we know anything about stars if they’re so far away and live for millions of years?

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.420
By being science ninjas!

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:31.920
HIYA!

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:32.700
Ninjas?

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:33.840
Yeah, ninjas are cool!

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.080
It doesn’t have to make sense!

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.980
So yes, stars are very far away, but they
send out light that travels the distance for us

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.420
and that light tells us a lot!

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.400
We need two pieces of information:
The type of light meaning its color

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.800
and the amount of light meaning its brightness.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.340
Contrary to popular belief, color is not a
property of matter.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.560
It’s a property of light.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.320
For normal things like you, me, and question
clone

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.080
our color depends on what light scatters off
of us.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.540
Turn off the lights and you don’t see any
color.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.100
Stars are different though.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.380
They make their own light.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.120
And the color of that light depends on the star’s
temperature.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.640
Let’s take a look at the most familiar star
in the sky: the Sun.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.470
It’s what we call a yellow-white dwarf meaning
its color is yellowish-white

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.200
and its size is modest by star standards.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.280
Or you might say Starn-dards?

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.100
Ey?

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.240
That yellowish-white color is on its chromosphere
meaning “color sphere,”

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.420
which has a temperature of about 5800 Kelvin.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.520
Let’s be clear though.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.340
Anything with a temperature above absolute
zero is making its own light.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.620
So basically everything.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.940
It just isn’t always visible to human eyes.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.760
Whenever particles nudge each other, a little
energy gets lost as light.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.560
What kind of light that is depends on the
how fast the particles are going

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.320
and, since particle motion tends to be pretty
random,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.680
objects with lots of particles emit lots of
different kinds of light.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.480
The human body is around 310 Kelvin, so most
of the light is infrared.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.340
For a light bulb filament or the Sun,
there is a lot more visible light.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.240
But if it’s losing energy, won’t it cool
down?

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.800
If it were an inanimate object, yes, but it’s
not.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.880
The human body uses chemistry to replace
that thermal energy.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.660
After all, humans are just sacks of aqueous
chemicals.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.580
A star, on the other hand, replaces any lost
energy through nuclear fusion in its core.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.900
The temperature in the Sun’s core is 15
million Kelvin,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.920
which is, like, so hot.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.180
Anyway, if you combine the brightness of the
star with its color,

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.380
you get what we call an HR Diagram.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.920
The Sun is yellowish-white, so it’s right
about here on the chart,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.660
but, if we plot a bunch of stars on here at
the same time, a pattern develops.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.160
These are the younger stars fusing hydrogen
into helium.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.480
These are the older stars fusing helium into
larger elements

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.060
and these are what are left over after small
stars die.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.320
You can draw a stars entire life on this chart.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.360
So, what’s a year in the life of a star?

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.720
Basically nothing.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.360
Big stars live for millions of years,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.420
the Sun will live for billions of years,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.440
and little red dwarfs can live for trillions
of years.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.360
Stars age so slowly that we’ve only seen
a few actually change.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.200
Then how do we know anything about their lives?

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.000
Because of that HR Diagram.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.799
We see the patterns in this diagram
and combine it with what we know about nuclear

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:17.480
fusion and plasmas
to figure out how old a star is.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.930
Then we put the different examples on a timeline
to get a full picture.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:24.080
Oh, right, that’s a great analogy.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.980
Imagine a group of aliens like Milton wanted
study the human lifespan,

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:30.290
but they could only spend a few seconds on
Earth.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.580
They would show up, take a bunch of pictures,
and leave.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.439
Working really hard to piece the pictures
together later.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.340
That’s exactly what we do with stars.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.320
Pretty crazy, huh?

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.519
So, will you ever look a star the same way
again?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.680
Let us know in the comments.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.379
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.780
Don’t forget to subscribe if you want to
keep up with us.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.780
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.180
In the last video, we learned about nuclear
fusion.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.440
Comment response time!

00:03:55.449 --> 00:03:59.099
Gastón Astore asked why we don’t shape
the Tokamak reactor like an 8 instead of a

00:03:59.100 --> 00:03:59.740
circle.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.200
Wouldn’t an 8-shape smash more together
at the center?

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.940
Well, maybe, but a circle is easier to make.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.080
Not just for the reactor itself, but also
the magnetic field.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.780
The reactor uses magnetic force to confine
the plasma

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.280
and plasma like any other charged particle
just naturally travels a circle in a magnetic field.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.600
It’s already a huge challenge to confine
the plasma in the first place.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.040
We don’t need to be making it any harder.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.180
Takezo Shinmen was wondering how dangerous
hydrogen fusion would be

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.000
and the answer is it’s actually really safe.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.380
Sure, the first time we figured it out, we
turned it into a bomb,

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.160
but that’s what happens when the military
funds your project.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.580
Plus you can turn most things that release
energy into a bomb,

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.620
so that doens't really count as a complaint.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.660
You have to put safeguards in place to prevent
explosion,

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:44.860
which is something we already do with the
current sources of energy.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.620
The best part about fusion though is that
it doesn’t create dangerous exhaust.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.540
It produces helium, which we’re in dire
need of anyway for cooling systems.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.720
We actually need the byproducts.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.160
Abu Sayed wanted to know about quantum tunneling
during fusion.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.720
OK, so I purposely avoided this in the video.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.500
My videos are already information-dense enough.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.100
Talking about quantum tunneling would have
distracted from what I was trying to say,

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.820
but we'll talk about it here for a bit.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.500
Hydrogen fusion begins at 13,000,000 Kelvin.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.300
It’s just that, on paper, if you only consider
the motion of the protons

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.640
you need hundreds of times higher temperatures.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.960
So, we’re clearly missing something if we
do that.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.260
If we also consider the protons are quantum
particles,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.760
which is exactly what they are.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.300
Then you don’t actually have to break the
coulomb barrier.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.260
You just have to get them close enough for
the uncertainty in their positions overlap.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:36.520
Dave Long asked how cosmic rays make things
like Lithium and Beryllium.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:41.300
So, yes, nuclear fusion skips over a few of
the early elements on the periodic table,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.440
but fission can come back and make them later.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.520
Fusion is when you combine small elements to make larger ones.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.720
Fission is when you take large elements and
break them apart to make smaller ones.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.960
Interstellar space has lots of loose particles
zipping around called Cosmic Rays.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.580
It’s a terrible name because they’re particles
not rays.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.500
Those particles are moving really fast and
occasionally smash into large elements

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.920
and the little pieces that break off are sometimes
Lithium and Beryllium.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.440
Lots of great questions this time.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.780
Anyway, thanks for watching. See
you next time.

